The project package facade exposes a project's public API to external consumers. It is the `@package.moth` file at the selected project root. That file is an ordinary support root of the project's structural module, not a separate facade node.

### The package surface

One optional `@package.moth` at the selected project directory is the external project package surface. Package assembly selects that support interface. It never falls back to the normal `@mod.moth` facade.

The directory may also contain `@mod.moth`, `@entry.moth` and `config.moth`. The project can be an application and a package at the same time.

The support root defines and exports its own API-only declarations: functions, structs, choices, type aliases, traits and compile-time constants. Helpers are shared with the other roots, but no root can import another root's local declarations.

### Facade restrictions

The facade:

- never bypasses an `export:` boundary
- is invisible to internal project modules
- cannot depend on `@project`
- cannot expose a semantic fact that depends on project-private context
- rejects top-level runtime work, direct fragments and builder-slot contributions
- has no implicit start and no route
- never becomes an application entry

Support roots and the project package facade reject root runtime and page fragments rather than merely staying inactive. The facade has no entry lifecycle to activate.

### Project package assembly

The compiler produces one immutable module artefact with optional normal and support interfaces. `ProjectPackageAssembly` is a separate link plan over:

- the selected support interface
- selected descendant public interfaces
- reachable generated functions
- package runtime requirements permitted by the target

Assembly never recompiles or mutates the facade.

Without a project-root `@package.moth`, the project has no externally consumable Moth package surface, even when it has `@mod.moth`.

The facade package identity comes from the `project` record's `name` field.

### External package eligibility

The build system rejects any declaration whose public semantic facts or reachable executable implementation directly or transitively depend on private `@project`. This includes an exported function that calls a private project-dependent helper.

The validator does not treat implementation-only dependence as a reusable package specialisation mechanism.

### Registered package surfaces

Only the external project package needs a project-root `@package.moth`. Registered Core and Builder source-backed packages keep their existing normal-or-support public-root contract. For example, `packages/html/@mod.moth` and `packages/web/site/@mod.moth` publish normal surfaces without a `@package` root.

Importing a package never activates its entry. A builder that registers a source package publishes that package's declared API surface, not a project facade.

### Dependency package facades

A dependency package exposes its facade and immutable artefacts to consumers. Consumers never see the dependency's private `@project`.

No declaration exposed through a dependency package facade may directly or transitively depend on that dependency's private `@project`. Private declarations may use the dependency's own `@project` only when no external package export can reach or expose them.

Configuration namespaces are scoped to one project or package compilation boundary. A consuming command's unqualified CLI or programmatic inputs do not implicitly satisfy a dependency's `$config` contracts.

